    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\r\n\r\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\r\n\r\n### Module(s)\r\n\r\nmini.comment\r\n\r\n### Description\r\n\r\nHello, I know markdown doesn't really have \"comments\", but it is kind of a consensus to respect HTML style comments ( `<!-- comment -->` ) on markdown files. That's the way tpope's plugin does it for example.\r\n\r\nCurrently mini.comment uses `# comment` for md files, and in markdown that means a header, so kinda the opposite of a comment.\r\n\r\n### Neovim version\r\n\r\nNVIM v0.10.0-dev\r\n\r\n### Steps to reproduce\r\n\r\n1. `nvim a.md`\r\n2. Write line\r\n3. gcc that line\r\n\r\n### Expected behavior\r\n\r\nComment format is `<!-- comment -->`\r\n\r\n### Actual behavior\r\n\r\nComment format is `# comment`",
